// The firewall question in Cedar's own vocabulary: `ipaddr`, not a classification field.
//
// Nothing in Dogwood's tree exercises this. `ip(` and `isInRange` appear in zero .dw files --
// no corpus case, no example, no test -- and `dogwood replay` cannot supply an address at all:
// its log parser has no case for an extension value, so `ip("10.1.2.3")` in a trace becomes the
// STRING `ip("10.1.2.3")`, the extension call fails on the wrong type, and the forbid silently
// does not apply. A policy that should DENY 10.1.2.3 replays as ALLOW.
//
// So this is checked by property rather than by replay, and the containment itself is
// differentially tested against Python's `ipaddress` module.

forbid (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Connect",
    resource
)
when { context.input.src.isInRange(ip("10.0.0.0/8")) };

permit ( principal, action == Anchor::Action::"Connect", resource );
